    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#pope-innocent-iv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Innocent IV" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C_o_a_Fieschi_Popes.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Echando una mano, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Lithuanian tribes in the forests between the Neman and the Neris remained pagan long after their neighbours converted, organised under dukes whose number is suggested by a treaty of 1219 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#galicia">Galicia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volhynia">Volhynia</a> that names twenty-one of them. Pressure from the crusading orders encouraged unification: in 1236 a force of Samogitians and other Lithuanians annihilated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> at the Battle of Saule, the event from which the state is conventionally dated here. Mindaugas, the most powerful of the dukes, eliminated or exiled his rivals and by the 1240s ruled a unified realm. Seeking peace with the Livonian Order, he accepted baptism in 1251 and was crowned king in 1253 with the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a>, the only crowned king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lithuania">Lithuania</a> ever had. His assassination in 1263 by rival dukes ended the kingdom and the first Christian experiment, and a period of instability followed. Under Traidenis and later Vytenis the state regained strength, and around 1316 Vytenis's brother Gediminas, who gave his name to the Gediminid dynasty, succeeded to a grand duchy that was ready to expand into the lands of Rus' weakened by the Mongol invasions. Gediminas made Vilnius his capital, and in letters of 1323 he invited merchants, craftsmen and clergy from the West to settle in his realm.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sigismund-ii-augustus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus.jpg" alt="Sigismund II Augustus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cranach_the_Younger_Sigismund_II_Augustus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Younger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy was a land of forest, lakes and rivers where most people farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rye">rye</a>, kept livestock and paid dues in kind to their lords. Its ethnic and religious make-up changed dramatically with expansion: by the fifteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slavs">Slavs</a>, ancestors of today's Belarusians and Ukrainians, formed the large majority, while ethnic Lithuanians lived mainly in the north-west. Pagan Lithuanians worshipped deities such as the thunder god Perkūnas and cremated their dead until conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#catholicism">Catholicism</a> began in 1387 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lithuania">Lithuania</a> proper and in 1413–1417 in Samogitia. Vytautas granted privileges to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a> in 1388 and settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatars</a> and Karaites near Trakai, communities whose descendants still live in Lithuania. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fur-trade">Furs</a>, wax, honey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a>, potash and later grain flowed down the rivers to Riga, Danzig and Königsberg. In 1557 the Volok Reform of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> reorganised royal estates into standard plots, increasing grain output but tightening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#serfdom">serfdom</a>. Vilnius, granted Magdeburg rights in 1387, grew into a multi-faith city of Catholic, Orthodox and later Protestant churches and synagogues, and by the eighteenth century it was renowned as a centre of Jewish learning, the home of the scholar known as the Vilna Gaon. Noble landowners, Lithuanian and Ruthenian, came to dominate rural society, while towns remained relatively small and many were owned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnates</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#algirdas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/algirdas.jpg" alt="Algirdas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#algirdas">Algirdas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Algirdas_kunigaik%C5%A1tis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">en:Alexander Guagnini (1538-1614), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s rise rested on mobile armies of light cavalry able to raid deep into enemy territory and on the defensive strength of forests, marshes and fortresses such as Kaunas, Trakai and Vilnius. For more than a century it fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the Livonian Order, answering their annual raids with counter-raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#livonia">Livonia</a>. At the same time it expanded eastwards: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algirdas">Algirdas</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> at the Battle of Blue Waters around 1362, opening the way to Kiev and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#podolia">Podolia</a>, and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moscow">Moscow</a> three times between 1368 and 1372. Vytautas suffered a heavy defeat against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatar</a> commander Edigu at the Battle of the Vorskla River in 1399, but in 1410 his troops fought beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poles">Poles</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> at the Battle of Grunwald, breaking the power of the Teutonic Order. From the late fifteenth century the main enemy was Muscovy, which won the Battle of Vedrosha in 1500 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#smolensk">Smolensk</a> in 1514, despite the Lithuanian victory under Konstanty Ostrogski at the Battle of Orsha the same year. After 1569 the grand duchy kept its own army under a grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hetman">hetman</a>, and in the seventeenth century its forces fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cossacks">Cossacks</a>, suffering a Russian occupation of Vilnius from 1655 to 1661.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duke held wide powers as war leader, judge and owner of much of the land, ruling through members of his dynasty and through local princes. Conquered Ruthenian principalities such as Polotsk, Kiev and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volhynia">Volhynia</a> were long left under Gediminid princes or local dynasties who kept their customs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> faith and Ruthenian law, a flexible approach summed up in a later saying that the Lithuanians neither changed the old nor introduced the new. Ruthenian, a written East Slavic language, served as the language of the chancery, whose copybooks of charters and decisions, the Lithuanian Metrica, survive as a major archive. After the Union of Horodło in 1413 the core was divided into the voivodeships of Vilnius and Trakai, and Catholic nobles received privileges and coats of arms on the Polish model. The Council of Lords became a powerful body under often absent Jagiellonian rulers, and a general diet developed in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. The reforms of 1564–1566 created new voivodeships and local dietines, extending political rights to the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gentry">gentry</a>, Orthodox as well as Catholic. Law was codified in three Statutes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lithuania">Lithuania</a> (1529, 1566 and 1588), among the most advanced legal codes of their time; the third remained in force in parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> until 1840.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#kievan-rus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kievan-rus.jpg" alt="Kievan Rus'" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_of_Kyivan_Rus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vitaliyf261, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy shaped the history of four modern nations. For Lithuanians it is the age of independence and greatness; for Belarusians, whose ancestors made up much of its population and whose language descends from that of its chancery, it is a formative state of their own; and for Ukrainians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poles">Poles</a> it is part of a shared past. By drawing most of the western lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> away from Mongol and later Muscovite control, it created a lasting divide between these lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a>, with consequences for later religious and national identities. Its dynastic union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a>, begun at Krewo in 1385, produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a>, which at times ruled Poland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hungary">Hungary</a>, and ultimately the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. The Statutes of Lithuania influenced legal thought across eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Cultural life drew on several traditions: Francysk Skaryna printed books in Vilnius in the 1520s, the first book in Lithuanian, Martynas Mažvydas's catechism, appeared at Königsberg in 1547, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jesuits">Jesuit</a> academy founded in 1579, now Vilnius University, became a leading centre of learning. The Union of Brest of 1596, which created the Ruthenian Uniate Church, and the long coexistence of Catholics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Protestants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tatars">Tatars</a> and Karaites made the grand duchy one of Europe's most religiously diverse states.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#livonian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livonian-war.jpg" alt="Livonian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#livonian-war">Livonian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siege_of_Narva_1558.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Boris Chorikov, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1386 the grand duchy shared a ruler with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a> for most of its history, though Vytautas, who ruled from 1392 to 1430, kept it practically independent and nearly obtained a royal crown. Growing danger from Muscovy, especially during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> took Polotsk in 1563, pushed the Lithuanian nobility towards closer union. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> transferred Podlasie, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#volhynia">Volhynia</a>, Kiev and Bracław to the Crown of Poland, and the grand duchy entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> with a common monarch, diet and foreign policy but its own army, treasury, offices and law. Polonisation of the nobility followed, and in 1696 Polish replaced Ruthenian as the language of administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#the-deluge">The Deluge</a> of 1655–1660 brought Russian and Swedish invasions, devastation and heavy population losses, and in the eighteenth century the grand duchy, like the whole Commonwealth, fell under Russian influence, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnate</a> families such as the Radziwiłłs and Sapiehas often more powerful than the state. The Reciprocal Guarantee of Two Nations, attached to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a>, reaffirmed its status, but the partitions of 1772, 1793 and 1795 divided its lands, almost all of which went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Grand Duchy of Lithuania grew from a pagan Baltic realm into one of the largest states of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, ruling most of present-day Belarus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and through its union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a> it shaped the religious, legal and national history of eastern Europe for more than five centuries.</em></p>

